WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:25.430
Olive Oatman: Life among the Mohave

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:30.279
Even those who aren't familiar with the name
"Olive Oatman" are undoubtedly familiar with

00:00:30.279 --> 00:00:31.279
her photo.

00:00:31.279 --> 00:00:35.760
The young, dark-haired girl with the solemn
expression and distinctive chin tattoo has

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:41.550
become synonymous with the hardships faced
by the pioneers who set out as a part of America's

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:47.440
Westward Expansion, a mass migration that
helped shape the middle of the 19th century.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:51.730
Even as thousands of intrepid settlers made
their way across the Midwestern plains on

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:56.620
the Oregon Trail, groups of Mormon pilgrims
headed to another destination: Salt Lake City.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:01.339
And still others headed deep into the southwestern
deserts for reasons that were entirely their

00:01:01.339 --> 00:01:05.470
own — and that is where the Oatman family
was going when they were waylaid at the place

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:10.900
now memorialized with a single, plain sign
that reminds travellers what happened there:

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:12.430
the Oatman Massacre.

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:17.120
The teenage Olive survived the attack that
killed most of her family, and experienced

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:19.610
a life that few lived to tell of.

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:23.810
She's most famous for her time spent among
the Native American tribes of the American

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:30.210
southwest, but her story begins much earlier
than that: with visions, prophecy, and religious

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:31.210
fervor.

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:36.880
The road to Hell is paved with good intentions

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:42.050
Emigrants who packed up everything and left
the relative comfort of the East Coast for

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:45.890
the wild landscape of the West did so for
all kinds of reasons.

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:48.760
Some looked for new opportunities, some were
just out for adventure, and some, well, it

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:53.520
wasn't so much about where they were going
but what they wanted to leave behind.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:57.950
For the Oatman family, their foray into the
Western frontier began with the revelations

00:01:57.950 --> 00:01:59.020
of a prophet.

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:05.150
Roys Oatman and his wife, Mary Ann, had seven
children between 1834 and 1849.

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:09.340
It was also during those years that the Methodist
family converted to Mormonism, after being

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:13.150
inspired by the prophecies and texts revealed
by Joseph Smith.

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:17.010
For those not familiar with the foundations
of Mormonism, it's worth a quick aside to

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:22.160
understand just why the Oatman family would
leave the relative safety of Vermont and head

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:23.160
west.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:27.060
Smith claimed to have found a set of golden
plates that he was able to translate with

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.090
the help of an angel.

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:33.190
He preached that it was their duty to seek
out the City of Zion, and along the way, they

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:35.230
met with a lot of resistance.

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:39.410
Believers were tarred and feathered, run out
of town, and persecuted for their beliefs.

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:43.150
And there were a lot of believers — including
Roys and his family.

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:47.560
In 1844, Smith announced he was going to run
for the presidency; however, not all those

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:52.250
who considered themselves Mormon agreed with
the move, or all of his teachings, for that

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:53.250
matter.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:56.960
One of the most controversial was polygamy,
and it was enough to splinter his followers

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:57.960
into other groups.

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:02.360
After Smith destroyed a press run by one of
these rival factions, he called on the local

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:03.360
militia for help.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:08.560
He was arrested, jailed, and on June 27, a
mob broke into the jail and killed him.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:12.570
One of the rival factions that had been such
a thorn in Smith's side was headed by a boy

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:14.021
named James Colin Brewster.

00:03:14.021 --> 00:03:18.630
Brewster had been only 10 years old when rumors
of his gift of prophecy began to circulate,

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:24.130
and at first, the mainstream Mormon elders
believed he was a genuine seer.

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:28.540
Buoyed by this, Brewster started bringing
the revelations fast and furious, ultimately

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:32.250
writing — with the help of his father — a
text he called the book of Esdras.

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:35.040
Included in the text was a description of
the promised land.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:39.209
It was a land destined to be the gathering
place of saints, and it was called Cedonia,

00:03:39.209 --> 00:03:41.050
the Land of Bashan.

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:46.460
Brewster claimed that the land was in a remote
corner of the country, somewhere in the southwest.

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:50.370
Smith and the mainstream church denounced
Brewster as a fraud, and Brewster gathered

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:54.470
his own following — appropriately, they
were called the Brewsterites, and more formally,

00:03:54.470 --> 00:03:56.070
they were the Church of Christ.

00:03:56.070 --> 00:04:00.520
The Oatman family were among Brewster's followers,
and at the time Smith was killed, the young

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:02.880
would-be prophet was just 17 years old.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:07.209
But that, many decided, was old enough to
lead the faithful to the promised land — even

00:04:07.209 --> 00:04:12.080
though he had been officially excommunicated
from the Mormon church before Smith died.

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:17.090
His prophecies began to get more specific,
and he claimed their final destination was

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:21.509
on the river "Amli", which he claimed was
another name for the Rio Grande.

00:04:21.509 --> 00:04:26.530
So it came to pass that on August 5, 1850,
the Oatman family left Missouri in a group

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:27.650
of about 90 people.

00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:32.300
There were 17 different families in their
little caravan, made up of 27 wagons, a few

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:36.400
hundred cattle, and — as it would turn out
— quite a bit of strife.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:42.300
Just a few more names among the thousands
of dead

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:45.889
Today, traveling across the country can be
done in a day.

00:04:45.889 --> 00:04:51.229
AIrlines are uncomfortable and inconvenient
and one of those modern luxuries no one really

00:04:51.229 --> 00:04:56.289
likes, but it's almost an infinite improvement
over the conditions in which the Oatman family

00:04:56.289 --> 00:05:00.770
— and thousands of other families — needed
to try to survive on their trek westward.

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:04.590
One in ten people who set out just on the
Oregon Trail didn't survive.

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:09.110
It was an active route for only 25 years,
but it still has the dubious distinction of

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:14.879
being America's longest graveyard with around
65,000 graves scattered along the way — that's

00:05:14.879 --> 00:05:19.919
one burial every 50 yards from Missouri to
Oregon City.

00:05:19.919 --> 00:05:22.279
There was no shortage of ways to die.

00:05:22.279 --> 00:05:26.289
Disease was rampant, and in some cases, a
person who was perfectly healthy in the morning

00:05:26.289 --> 00:05:27.729
would be dead by dusk.

00:05:27.729 --> 00:05:31.699
There was cholera and scurvy, malaria, and
of course, there was always the possibility

00:05:31.699 --> 00:05:36.650
of snakebites, falls, broken bones, infections,
and accidental gunshot wounds — something

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:38.779
that happened more often than you might expect.

00:05:38.779 --> 00:05:43.229
Just imagine the hardships: surviving on nothing
more than the dried food you could carry and

00:05:43.229 --> 00:05:44.319
what you could hunt.

00:05:44.319 --> 00:05:46.930
Dust and dirt that infected every nook and
cranny.

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:50.870
And — if you were among the later settlers
that set off — you were following in the

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:55.169
footsteps of thousands of other people and
animals… and trekking through all the waste

00:05:55.169 --> 00:05:56.759
they left in their wake.

00:05:56.759 --> 00:06:02.080
It's truly not surprising that tempers might
be running wild, and that conflict within

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:06.389
groups who needed to stay together for safety
might be commonplace.

00:06:06.389 --> 00:06:10.099
Things only got worse for the Brewsterites
as they left the plains and headed into the

00:06:10.099 --> 00:06:15.069
mountains of New Mexico, traveling a route
known to the natives as Jornada Del Muerto,

00:06:15.069 --> 00:06:16.340
or the Journey of Death.

00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:20.449
It wasn't just difficult terrain, either — it
was one of the longest, hottest, and driest

00:06:20.449 --> 00:06:23.999
years on record, and those that lived there
were getting desperate.

00:06:23.999 --> 00:06:28.680
As the caravan of devout families traveled,
they passed more and more ominous landmarks:

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:33.569
human remains, picked clean by scavengers,
dead and discarded pack animals, goods and

00:06:33.569 --> 00:06:36.289
garbage that oxen were no longer strong enough
to carry.

00:06:36.289 --> 00:06:37.289
Native tribes tested, every night — the
caravan would wake to find some of their animals

00:06:37.289 --> 00:06:38.289
gone, and fear was nearly tangible.

00:06:38.289 --> 00:06:39.289
Fear among those who had faith not quite as
strong as Brewster's, that is: even as workers

00:06:39.289 --> 00:06:40.289
on the US mail train warned them that it wasn't
safe, he insisted their faith would protect

00:06:40.289 --> 00:06:41.289
them from whatever dangers they might face.

00:06:41.289 --> 00:06:42.289
So, you might be able to see why the Oatman
family only traveled with the rest of the

00:06:42.289 --> 00:06:43.289
Brewerites as far as Las Vegas.

00:06:43.289 --> 00:06:46.949
That, historians say, is when the party split:
26 people, including the Oatmans and three

00:06:46.949 --> 00:06:51.319
other families, set off in the direction of
California, while the others followed their

00:06:51.319 --> 00:06:52.319
prophet.

00:06:52.319 --> 00:06:56.990
The splinter group headed along the El Camino
Real, and in January of 1851, they made camp

00:06:56.990 --> 00:06:57.990
at La Joya.

00:06:57.990 --> 00:07:02.569
They worked for a bit cutting and delivering
hay, before moving on toward Maricopa Wells.

00:07:02.569 --> 00:07:05.059
By this time, they knew they were being followed.

00:07:05.059 --> 00:07:09.349
Cooler heads prevailed, at first, and the
group decided to wait at the Pima villages

00:07:09.349 --> 00:07:13.879
until they were joined by travellers who were
inevitably coming along behind them.

00:07:13.879 --> 00:07:17.360
Greater numbers would have made them a less
tempting target, but again, it's possible

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:22.219
to see why they would just want to get on
with their journey and reach their destination.

00:07:22.219 --> 00:07:26.069
After three weeks of waiting, Roys decided
that he and his family — including his wife,

00:07:26.069 --> 00:07:29.050
who was due to give birth any day — were
going to push on.

00:07:29.050 --> 00:07:32.389
They were going to be doing it alone, too,
as no other families would join them.

00:07:32.389 --> 00:07:35.830
Their destination was Fort Yuma, 190 miles
away.

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:41.389
That doesn't sound far, in our 21st century
world of wide roads and fast cars, but the

00:07:41.389 --> 00:07:45.779
Oatman family would have been trundling along
at a pace that was, at the fastest, between

00:07:45.779 --> 00:07:47.610
15 and 20 miles per day.

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:52.360
A lot can happen in that kind of time, and
things did happen.

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:53.649
Horrible things.

00:07:53.649 --> 00:07:57.520
The Oatmans decided to take a shortcut, but
they were slowed down even more.

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:01.479
Their oxen were ill-suited to the terrain
they found themselves crossing, and they were

00:08:01.479 --> 00:08:04.689
struggling to get up and down the rocky hills
and plateaus.

00:08:04.689 --> 00:08:08.199
On February 18, 1851, they spotted a large
group approaching.

00:08:08.199 --> 00:08:11.789
There were between 17 and 20 — members of
a Native American tribe that have variously

00:08:11.789 --> 00:08:12.789
been called Apache, Yavapai, and Maricopa.

00:08:12.789 --> 00:08:16.069
The details of exactly how the confrontation
happened are understandably scarce, but it's

00:08:16.069 --> 00:08:19.960
believed Roys approached the group, who demanded
tobacco and pipes.

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:23.689
That, Roys shared, but when they demanded
food, and were not satisfied with the bread

00:08:23.689 --> 00:08:27.919
he offered, he told them that he didn't have
enough to feed his own family, much less them.

00:08:27.919 --> 00:08:32.099
The conflict escalated, and by the time it
was over, all but three were dead.

00:08:32.099 --> 00:08:36.029
In her later memoirs, Olive Oatman described
seeing the broken and bloody bodies of her

00:08:36.029 --> 00:08:37.870
family members, scattered across the ground.

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:39.080
Then, she fell into unconsciousness.

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:44.080
Olive and her younger sister, Mary Ann, weren't
killed — they were taken captive, and when

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:48.579
they woke, they spent several days on a march
to the village of their captors.

00:08:48.579 --> 00:08:51.709
Almost immediately, they were stripped of
their shoes — making it almost impossible

00:08:51.709 --> 00:08:53.810
to walk on their bloody and bruised feet,
much less try to run away.

00:08:53.810 --> 00:08:57.509
Olive wrote of those first few days, when
the grief was fresh and they were still coming

00:08:57.509 --> 00:09:00.790
to terms with the fact that everything had
changed in a heartbeat.

00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:04.930
She wrote of stopping to make camp, of watching
a cooking fire built, saying:

00:09:04.930 --> 00:09:09.680
"They boiled some of the beans just from our
wagon, mixed some flour with water, and baked

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:10.790
it in the ashes.

00:09:10.790 --> 00:09:14.740
They offered us some food, but in the most
insulting and taunting manner, continually

00:09:14.740 --> 00:09:19.680
making merry over every indication of grief
in us, and with which our hearts were ready

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:20.700
to break.

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:21.750
We could not eat."

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:25.770
There was one more survivor: Lorenzo, who
has an important part to play in his sisters'

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:26.770
story.

00:09:26.770 --> 00:09:31.100
He survived only by sheer luck, later describing
waking "with the mangled remains of my own

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:37.470
parents lying near by, my scalp torn open,
my person covered with blood, alone, friendless,

00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:42.740
in a wild, mountain, dismal, wilderness region,
exposed to the ravenous beasts, and more,

00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:47.199
to the ferocity of more than brutal savages
and human-shaped demons."

00:09:47.199 --> 00:09:51.540
Incredibly, he managed to make his way back
to the Pima village and the safety they had

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:54.440
left behind, joining up with another group
heading to California.

00:09:54.440 --> 00:09:58.220
Along the way, he and the family who had taken
him in stopped at the site of the massacre.

00:09:58.220 --> 00:10:02.210
The Oatmans were buried in a mass grave, and
Lorenzo would embark on a quest to find his

00:10:02.210 --> 00:10:19.490
sisters — who were not among the dead.

00:10:19.490 --> 00:11:52.290
From slave to treasure

00:11:52.290 --> 00:11:56.009
Mary Ann barely survived the march to the
village, carried part of the way by one of

00:11:56.009 --> 00:11:59.959
their captors when she grew so weak — and,
Olive later said, so "utterly fearless of

00:11:59.959 --> 00:12:01.870
death" — that she could no longer walk.

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:05.829
Nevertheless, they made it to camp: the arrival
of the raiding party was greeted with dancing,

00:12:05.829 --> 00:12:09.980
singing, and merry-making, and Olive's description
of life among them isn't for the faint of

00:12:09.980 --> 00:12:10.980
heart.

00:12:10.980 --> 00:12:14.160
They described a village where women were
"the laborers and principal burden-bearers,"

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:18.730
and where she and her sister were immediately
put to work as slaves; only the slightest

00:12:18.730 --> 00:12:23.230
excuse was needed for whippings and beatings
to be handed out, but it was the foreboding

00:12:23.230 --> 00:12:28.209
sense that something much worse was waiting
just around the next corner, just coming with

00:12:28.209 --> 00:12:30.690
the new dawn, that truly took a toll.

00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:31.690
Olive later wrote,

00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:36.540
"There was no beauty, no loveliness, no attractions
in the country possessed by these unlovely

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:41.889
creatures [...] Sometimes I would tell [Mary]
that I saw but little chance for escape; that

00:12:41.889 --> 00:12:47.439
we had better be good and ready for any fate,
and try to wait in submission for our lot."

00:12:47.439 --> 00:12:51.899
Months into their captivity, and Olive wrote
that they became more "merciful," in large

00:12:51.899 --> 00:12:56.940
part because of her sister, and in part because
of their skill at learning their captors'

00:12:56.940 --> 00:12:57.940
language.

00:12:57.940 --> 00:13:01.529
The women and children they met were particularly
fascinated with their stories of their home,

00:13:01.529 --> 00:13:03.459
of the ocean, and of their travels.

00:13:03.459 --> 00:13:07.670
Among themselves, though, Olive described
a spirit of "low obscenity and meanness,"

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:12.009
and they would regularly ask the girls if
they wanted to escape — a question they

00:13:12.009 --> 00:13:13.959
didn't dare answer honestly.

00:13:13.959 --> 00:13:17.399
Fortunately for the girls, a nearby group
of Mohave had been trading with this group

00:13:17.399 --> 00:13:18.399
for several years.

00:13:18.399 --> 00:13:21.260
And finally, they came — only after, Olive
said,

00:13:21.260 --> 00:13:26.439
"Months rolled by, finding us early and late
at our burden-bearing and torturing labors,

00:13:26.439 --> 00:13:31.790
plying hands and feet to heed the demands
of our lazy lords, and the taunts and exactions

00:13:31.790 --> 00:13:35.310
of a swarm of heathen urchins, sometimes set
over us."

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:39.560
The Mohave emissary was made up of five men
and one woman; her appearance gave the girls

00:13:39.560 --> 00:13:43.700
hope that if they were sold, they may find
their lot in life even slightly improved.

00:13:43.700 --> 00:13:48.560
And they were sold — the Mohave paid for
them in horses, blankets, and food.

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:52.100
They were on the move again, and ten days
later, they came to the Mohave village.

00:13:52.100 --> 00:13:54.220
Here, Olive wrote, they found a home.

00:13:54.220 --> 00:13:58.019
They were welcomed into the chief's home,
and taken into the care of his wife.

00:13:58.019 --> 00:14:02.060
The woman who had negotiated for their purchase
had been the chief's daughter, and her sympathy

00:14:02.060 --> 00:14:07.199
toward their plight gave them the better life
they hadn't dared to dream of, much less speak

00:14:07.199 --> 00:14:08.199
of.

00:14:08.199 --> 00:14:11.189
While the sisters had been shocked at their
captors' lifestyle and their apparent inability

00:14:11.189 --> 00:14:16.339
to survive on anything that they hadn't killed
or stolen, their Mohave family even gave them

00:14:16.339 --> 00:14:18.020
their own plot of land to grow crops.

00:14:18.020 --> 00:14:22.029
They were expected to contribute to the tribe's
welfare, and they did: they gathered wood,

00:14:22.029 --> 00:14:25.500
dug for roots, planted and harvested crops,
and foraged for berries.

00:14:25.500 --> 00:14:26.500
They made friends, too.

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:29.829
They were given nicknames, and the facial
tattoo that would make Olive's appearance

00:14:29.829 --> 00:14:33.199
so distinctive — and strange, in the eyes
of the world she had been born into.

00:14:33.199 --> 00:14:36.850
It was done to both Mary and Olive, and it
was a painful process.

00:14:36.850 --> 00:14:40.769
Their skin was pricked and a pigment made
from the powder of river rocks and the juice

00:14:40.769 --> 00:14:42.999
from weeds was rubbed into their bloody skin.

00:14:42.999 --> 00:14:46.970
Painful, yes, but it was a mark of friendship
and of respect.

00:14:46.970 --> 00:14:48.019
They were family.

00:14:48.019 --> 00:14:52.339
And as family, the Mohave did all they could
when Mary Ann grew weaker and weaker.

00:14:52.339 --> 00:14:56.440
She died in 1855, after a long illness caused
by a lack of nutrition.

00:14:56.440 --> 00:15:00.660
It wasn't just Mary Ann, either — drought
had made food scarce, and Olive later remembered

00:15:00.660 --> 00:15:04.899
traveling for days to find berries that would
sustain the dying for just a little longer.

00:15:04.899 --> 00:15:08.700
She helped tend to the sick, administered
medicine, and felt the devastation that came

00:15:08.700 --> 00:15:09.700
with failure.

00:15:09.700 --> 00:15:14.459
Then, she would travel for days again, searching
for bird eggs, for roots, for anything that

00:15:14.459 --> 00:15:17.930
could prolong her sister's life until the
rivers were filled with fish again.

00:15:17.930 --> 00:15:22.110
But for all her efforts, and the efforts of
the chief and his family, Mary Ann passed

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:23.110
away.

00:15:23.110 --> 00:15:28.110
"She sank into the sleep of death as quietly
as sinks the innocent infant to sleep in its

00:15:28.110 --> 00:15:29.310
mother's arms," Olive would later remember.

00:15:29.310 --> 00:15:33.559
While tradition dictated that the bodies of
the tribe's dead were to be cremated, the

00:15:33.559 --> 00:15:38.040
chief's wife appealed to her husband to allow
Olive to do as she wished with her sister's

00:15:38.040 --> 00:15:41.629
body, and she was ultimately buried according
to her own traditions.

00:15:41.629 --> 00:15:46.519
Now, Olive was truly alone, save the continued
kindness of the chief's wife and daughter,

00:15:46.519 --> 00:15:52.300
which she said sustained her more than any
bit of gruel or handful of corn.

00:15:52.300 --> 00:15:57.329
The desperate search of a heartbroken brother

00:15:57.329 --> 00:16:01.009
Years passed, and Lorenzo never gave up the
search for his sisters.

00:16:01.009 --> 00:16:05.949
He joined expeditions of explorers and prospectors,
he searched the length and breadth of southern

00:16:05.949 --> 00:16:11.720
California for even the slightest bit of information
or indeed, of hope they were still alive.

00:16:11.720 --> 00:16:16.139
He even petitioned the governor of California
for help, and got a response he hadn't wanted:

00:16:16.139 --> 00:16:18.779
the governor had no authority to do anything.

00:16:18.779 --> 00:16:22.899
Lorenzo's determination kept the girls' plight
the talk of the towns.

00:16:22.899 --> 00:16:28.080
And that meant he wasn't the only one looking
for his sisters; in 1853, a man named Henry

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:32.649
Grinnell passed through Fort Yuma and heard
the tale of the two young girls who had been

00:16:32.649 --> 00:16:33.930
taken captive.

00:16:33.930 --> 00:16:37.540
Finding those who were lost was in his blood:
he was the nephew of another Henry Grinnell,

00:16:37.540 --> 00:16:42.620
who used the profits from his shipping company
to fund the voyages that went in search of

00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:46.420
Sir John Franklin, who went missing during
his attempts to find the Northwest Passage.

00:16:46.420 --> 00:16:49.660
Our Grinnell was a carpenter, but an inquisitive
one.

00:16:49.660 --> 00:16:53.699
He asked so many questions for so long, that
he was finally approached by a Native American

00:16:53.699 --> 00:16:55.120
man who had some information.

00:16:55.120 --> 00:17:00.130
His name was Francisco, and he asked Grinnell
about the girls in January of 1856.

00:17:00.130 --> 00:17:03.459
Grinnell — who pretended to get his information
directly from the newspaper he was holding

00:17:03.459 --> 00:17:07.910
— told Francisco that the American government
knew there were two girls being held against

00:17:07.910 --> 00:17:09.000
their will somewhere in the southwest.

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:12.380
He added that they were prepared to go to
war to save them…

00:17:12.380 --> 00:17:15.260
unless, that is, their captors saw fit to
release them.

00:17:15.260 --> 00:17:20.190
Francisco promised to bring the girls to safety,
in exchange for a few blankets and some beads.

00:17:20.190 --> 00:17:23.700
He only asked for 20 days, and promised he
would return.

00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:27.600
Even as Grinnell was ridiculed for his faith
that the emissary would, indeed, do all he

00:17:27.600 --> 00:17:32.480
could to rescue the girls, Francisco really
did head off to the Mohave tribe where Olive

00:17:32.480 --> 00:17:33.480
still lived.

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:37.690
For her part, Olive knew that the attitudes
of the chief and his wife seemed to change,

00:17:37.690 --> 00:17:42.420
and finally, she came to realize that someone
was, indeed, trying to bargain for her release.

00:17:42.420 --> 00:17:46.050
The Mohave didn't want to let her go, though,
even going as far as to tell Francisco that

00:17:46.050 --> 00:17:47.890
this wasn't the girl he was looking for.

00:17:47.890 --> 00:17:52.760
She wasn't American, they insisted, she was
from a far away tribe that lived in the same

00:17:52.760 --> 00:17:54.570
direction as the setting sun.

00:17:54.570 --> 00:17:59.321
They told Olive to tell Francisco the same,
but when it came time for her to talk, she

00:17:59.321 --> 00:18:00.390
told the truth.

00:18:00.390 --> 00:18:04.800
She was threatened with torture, called a
liar, told they were simply not going to let

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:05.800
her go.

00:18:05.800 --> 00:18:09.640
But they did: a beautiful white horse was
added to seal the deal, and Olive visited

00:18:09.640 --> 00:18:13.130
her sister's grave one last time before she
left for Fort Yuma.

00:18:13.130 --> 00:18:17.350
Reacclimation wasn't easy for Olive; she had
transitioned almost completely from speaking

00:18:17.350 --> 00:18:21.270
English, and when she — accompanied by the
chief's daughter — returned to the fort,

00:18:21.270 --> 00:18:23.810
she was quickly given more "appropriate" clothing
to wear.

00:18:23.810 --> 00:18:28.010
She was given over to the care of women working
in the fort, and Lorenzo read about her return

00:18:28.010 --> 00:18:30.680
in the same paper Grinnell had pretended to
read from.

00:18:30.680 --> 00:18:34.500
Confirming it was true, he headed back to
Fort Yuma and they were quickly reunited.

00:18:34.500 --> 00:18:37.470
Here is about where Lorenzo's part in the
story ends.

00:18:37.470 --> 00:18:41.240
He traveled with his sister for a bit, living
with her in California and New York.

00:18:41.240 --> 00:18:46.650
He eventually married and got into the hotel
business, and died unexpectedly in 1901.

00:18:46.650 --> 00:18:50.070
As for Olive, she struggled to readjust to
the life she'd been taken from.

00:18:50.070 --> 00:18:54.380
She kept her promise to the Mohave: whenever
she spoke about them, she told stories of

00:18:54.380 --> 00:18:55.610
how kind they had been.

00:18:55.610 --> 00:19:00.090
She denied that she had ever suffered at their
hands, and after the Reverend Royal Byron

00:19:00.090 --> 00:19:04.110
Stratton wrote a slightly embellished account
about her life among the Mohave, she used

00:19:04.110 --> 00:19:08.670
a part of the profits from the book and her
time on the lecture circuit to send both her

00:19:08.670 --> 00:19:10.030
and her brother to college.

00:19:10.030 --> 00:19:12.050
In 1865, she married John Fairchild.

00:19:12.050 --> 00:19:15.840
They moved to Texas, and after 10 years, they
adopted a baby girl.

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:21.380
She died in 1903, and although she occasionally
used makeup to cover her tattoos for pictures,

00:19:21.380 --> 00:19:26.000
she never entirely turned her back on the
way of life taught to her by the Mohave.

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:30.040
Until she died, she always kept a jar of hazelnuts
in her kitchen — one of the staple foods

00:19:30.040 --> 00:19:47.890
she had once collected for her second family.

